 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "SYCAMORE GAP. For about 150 years a lone sycamore grew in this dip in the Wall, loved by everyone who saw it. It was cut down in 2023, and missed ever since. The stump has sent up new shoots."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "HOUSESTEADS. Roman Vercovicium, a fort for about 800 soldiers, built around AD 124. It has the best-preserved Roman toilets in Britain: a row of seats over a flushing channel. They sat and chatted."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "HADRIAN'S WALL. Begun around AD 122 for the Emperor Hadrian, it ran about 73 miles from the Tyne to the Solway, with a milecastle every Roman mile. Soldiers built it, and scratched their names in the stone."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "VINDOLANDA. Nearby, 1,900-year-old wooden tablets were found, including a birthday invitation from Claudia Severa to her friend Sulpicia Lepidina. It's the oldest known writing by a woman in Britain."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "hadrians_wall_whin_sill",
   "title": "The Whin Sill",
   "text": "The Whin Sill is a sheet of hard volcanic dolerite that squeezed up between older rocks about 295 million years ago. Here the Wall rides along its crags, a ready-made rampart. Bamburgh Castle and the Farne Islands stand on the very same rock."
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 45,
   "plaque": "hadrians_wall_tungrians",
   "title": "The Tungrians",
   "text": "Housesteads was garrisoned for much of its life by the First Cohort of Tungrians, soldiers raised in what is now Belgium. The Wall's garrisons came from all over the empire, from the Rhineland to Syria. Northumberland in February must have been quite a posting."
  }
 ],
 "spawn": [
  21,
  4
 ],
